========================================================================================================================
CANDIDATE K-B -- the external-field monopole: M_dyn,total / M_bar = nu(g_ext/a_0)?
========================================================================================================================

(A) THE COEFFICIENT -- what QUMOND actually gives for the monopole, by exact flux quadrature
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  QUMOND:  div(grad Phi) = div[ nu(|grad Phi_N|/a_0) grad Phi_N ].  Gauss over a sphere of radius r around a
  point baryonic mass M_b sitting in a uniform external Newtonian field g_ext:
      M_dyn(r) = (r^2 / 2G) * INT_{-1}^{+1} nu(|g|/a_0) (e + E mu) dmu ,
      e = G M_b/r^2,  E = g_ext,  |g| = sqrt(E^2 + 2 E e mu + e^2),  mu = cos(angle to the external field).
  This is a ONE-DIMENSIONAL quadrature of the exact field -- nothing is expanded.

   e_N = g_ext/a_0  CANDIDATE nu(e_N)   QUMOND flux (r -> inf)   nu_e(1+L_e/3)  candidate error (dex)
            0.0001           100.5008                  83.8343         83.8343                 0.0787
             0.001            32.1254                  26.8554         26.8554                 0.0778
            0.0033            17.9126                  15.0121         15.0121                 0.0767
              0.01            10.5083                   8.8431          8.8431                 0.0749
               0.1             3.6886                   3.1659          3.1659                 0.0664
                 1             1.5820                   1.4285          1.4285                 0.0443
                10             1.0442                   1.0199          1.0199                 0.0102
  [PASS] A1 the flux quadrature reproduces the analytic monopole coefficient nu_e(1 + L_e/3) to 0.2% at every e_N (an independent second implementation of hunt_2026/k03's theorem)   (max |flux/theory - 1| = 1.02e-09)
  [FAIL] A2 AGAINST THE CANDIDATE: the proposed coefficient nu(e_N) is NOT the QUMOND monopole coefficient.  The flux carries an extra factor (1 + L_e/3), which tends to 5/6 in a deep external field, so the candidate as written over-states the total dynamical mass by up to 0.079 dex   (max |log10 nu(e_N)/QUMOND| = 0.0787 dex over e_N = 1e-4..10; deep-limit factor 5/6 = 0.0792 dex.  THIS CHECK IS EXPECTED TO FAIL and its failure is the result.)
  [PASS] A3 the deep-external-field limit of the flux coefficient is exactly 5/6   (flux/nu_e -> 0.834165 (5/6 = 0.833333))

(B) THE RESTATEMENT TEST -- can this be derived from v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus algebra?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Step 1.  Deep-MOND limit:  g(r) = sqrt(G M_b a_0)/r  <=>  v^4 = G M_b a_0.  This alone says NOTHING about
           where the flat part ends, so M_dyn = v^2 r/G is undetermined -- the candidate is right about that.
  Step 2.  Add ONE more ingredient, the EFE truncation radius, itself pure algebra: the isolated internal
           field g_N = G M_b/r^2 equals the external field g_ext at r_EFE, i.e.
               r_EFE = sqrt(G M_b / g_ext) = sqrt(G M_b/a_0)/sqrt(e_N) = r_M / sqrt(e_N).
  Step 3.  M_dyn = g(r_EFE) r_EFE^2 / G = [sqrt(G M_b a_0)/r_EFE] r_EFE^2/G = sqrt(G M_b a_0) r_EFE / G
               = sqrt(G M_b a_0) * sqrt(G M_b/a_0) / (G sqrt(e_N)) = M_b / sqrt(e_N).
  Step 4.  And the deep limit of the kernel is nu(e_N) -> 1/sqrt(e_N).  SO THE TWO ARE THE SAME EXPRESSION.
  => The relation closes from the deep-MOND limit plus the definition of the EFE radius.  Nothing beyond
     v^4 = G M_b a_0 and dimensional algebra is used.  Verified numerically over the measured e_N range:

         e_N   nu(e_N) [kernel]   1/sqrt(e_N) [pure algebra]      ratio
      0.0001           100.5008                     100.0000    1.00501
     0.00038            51.8005                      51.2989    1.00978
       0.001            32.1254                      31.6228    1.01589
      0.0033            17.9126                      17.4078    1.02900
        0.01            10.5083                      10.0000    1.05083
  [PASS] B1 RESTATEMENT: over the whole range of external field the reconstruction actually measures (e_N = 1e-4 to 1e-2), the kernel form nu(e_N) and the pure-algebra form 1/sqrt(e_N) agree to better than 6%, so the candidate carries no information beyond v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus the EFE radius.  IS_RESTATEMENT = TRUE   (max |nu(e_N) sqrt(e_N) - 1| = 0.0508 over e_N = 1e-4..1e-2; the kernel's interpolation only differs at e_N ~ 1, which the measured fields never reach (max reconstructed e_N = 3.9e-2))

