  [FAIL] D1 THE TEST.  The candidate says the dynamical mass saturates at r_EFE, which for these lenses at the reconstructed external fields is 0.2-0.7 Mpc -- well inside the KiDS reach of 2.6 Mpc.  If the data show that saturation, the EFE model must fit BETTER than the isolated one.  This check passes only if it does (Delta chi2 < 0 in at least one bracket, both footings)   (canonical: Delta chi2 = +143.5 (no-cluster e_N), +507.2 (max-cluster e_N); alt: +169.2, +548.8.  Positive = the EFE truncation is DISFAVOURED by the data.)
  [FAIL] D2 the 3-sigma upper limit on a common external field from the KiDS lensing shape alone, with the amplitude free in every bin: e_N must be below the value quoted.  Compare with the reconstruction's median of 3.8e-4 (no-cluster) to 3.3e-3 (max-cluster) -- this check passes only if the lensing limit is ABOVE the reconstructed median, i.e. only if the two are compatible   (KiDS 3-sigma limit e_N < 6.81e-05; reconstruction medians 3.8e-4 / 3.3e-3)
  [PASS] D3 MUTATION CONTROL: Newtonian baryons (nu = 1) must be excluded outright by the same data and the same amplitude freedom, or the test has no power at all   (Delta chi2 (Newtonian - framework isolated) = +1061.9)

  THE CONFOUND THIS TEST MUST SURVIVE.  The mass that generates g_ext is real mass a few Mpc away, and it is
  correlated with the lens, so it contributes its own lensing signal at large R (the 'two-halo' term).  An EFE
  deficit in the lens's own profile could in principle be refilled by it.  So the test is repeated with a FREE
  correlated-structure component added to every model -- shape ESD ~ R^-0.8 over 0.1-3 Mpc, the projected
  linear correlation function's slope -- with its own free amplitude in each mass bin (8 parameters, not 4).
    + free two-halo, isolated framework            :  chi2 =     97.8   two-halo amplitudes = -0.402, -0.375, +0.822, -0.386
    + free two-halo, EFE at  no-cluster e_N = 3.82e-04:  chi2 =     87.5   Delta chi2 vs isolated =    -10.3   two-halo amplitudes = +0.770, +1.209, +2.009, +1.793
    + free two-halo, EFE at max-cluster e_N = 3.27e-03:  chi2 =    114.9   Delta chi2 vs isolated =    +17.1   two-halo amplitudes = +0.904, +1.769, +2.430, +2.552
  [FAIL] D5 AGAINST MY OWN RESULT ABOVE: the D1 exclusion is NOT robust to a correlated-structure term.  Allow four extra free parameters with the two-halo shape and the weak-field (no-cluster) EFE truncation stops being excluded and becomes mildly PREFERRED, because a rising two-halo term and a falling EFE deficit are nearly the same degree of freedom (bug pattern 5).  This check passes only if the EFE model stays excluded (Delta chi2 > 9) in BOTH brackets even with that freedom   (no-cluster: Delta chi2 = -10.3; max-cluster: Delta chi2 = +17.1.  So the honest statement is: KiDS excludes the truncation at the strong-field bracket (+17.1) and cannot decide it at the weak-field one (-10.3) once the neighbours that GENERATE g_ext are allowed to lens as well.  Note the required two-halo amplitudes are physical (positive) in the EFE fits.)

  the LambdaCDM alternative, computed beside the framework: NFW halo, M200 from a stellar-to-halo-mass
